message: >-
  If you use Fractal Intelligence in your research, please cite the
  preprint below.
title: "Fractal Intelligence: Conceptual Decomposition as Problem-Solving Infrastructure"
abstract: >-
  Instead of asking how a human would solve a problem, Fractal
  Intelligence asks what the problem is made of, decomposing it into
  concepts rather than tasks. Each concept becomes a node behind a
  uniform five-surface contract (the Solver), behind which any
  reasoner can stand: a model, a human, or a further subtree, so a
  collective presents to its parent as a single node. The boundary
  isolates as well as composes: rejection forces a re-carve rather
  than a retry, and concepts that recur become permanent,
  content-addressed infrastructure, so reasoning accumulates across
  problems rather than being rebuilt each time. The decisive test is
  whether decomposed solving outperforms the conventional approach at
  matched compute; the deeper question is whether independent
  decompositions converge on the same concepts.
